  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nHigh pressure at the surface and aloft will continue through the\nwork week, but as we head into the weekend, an approaching upper\nstorm system will push the high pressure to the east of the Quad\nState. Guidance continues to be non-committal for rain chances\nacross the region this weekend, but there is no sign of\nsignificant QPF. Surface high pressure will remain along the\nnorthern Gulf Coast, so moisture return will be very limited\nahead of the system. This forecast maintains the 20-30% chance\nof rainfall focused on late Saturday night into Sunday morning.\nA slight chance of thunder has entered the forecast over the\nBootheel and Purchase Area into southeast Illinois overnight\nSaturday night, but with the upper system well north of the \nregion, thunder will be very difficult to get.\n\nPlentiful sunshine, south/southeast winds, and dry air will\nallow temperatures to climb above normal today and trend even\nhigher through the rest of the work week. Highs should reach the\nupper 60s Thursday through Saturday, and there is a 30-70%\nchance of reaching 70 degrees Friday and Saturday. That would be\n10-15 degrees above normal levels, which are in the mid to upper\n50s. \n\nLooking ahead to next week (Thanksgiving week), a cold frontal\npassage Sunday will lead to a cool start to the week with\ntemperatures slightly below normal. However, with the flow aloft\nbecoming more zonal and progressive, temperatures should rebound\nwith a strong signal for above normal temperatures through the \nholiday weekend. The more progressive flow will make it \ndifficult to pin down any prospects for rainfall, but there are \nno signs of any significant storm systems at this time.",
